# Codexion

## Description

Codexion is a concurrency simulation where several coders share a limited
number of USB dongles to compile their code. Each coder is represented by a
thread that cycles through compiling, debugging and refactoring. Compiling
requires holding two dongles at the same time, one in each hand. If a coder
does not start compiling within `time_to_burnout` milliseconds, they burn out
and the simulation stops.

The goal is to coordinate access to the shared dongles while avoiding deadlock,
starvation and burnout, using POSIX threads, mutexes, condition variables and a
custom priority queue for fair scheduling.

## Instructions

Compile with:

    make

Run with:

    ./codexion number_of_coders time_to_burnout time_to_compile time_to_debug time_to_refactor number_of_compiles_required dongle_cooldown scheduler

- All time values are in milliseconds.
- `scheduler` must be either `fifo` or `edf`.

Example:

    ./codexion 5 2000 200 200 200 10 0 fifo

The simulation stops when a coder burns out, or when every coder has compiled at
least `number_of_compiles_required` times.

## Blocking cases handled

- **Deadlock prevention.** A deadlock can occur if every coder grabs one dongle
  and waits for the other, forming a circular wait (one of Coffman's four
  conditions). To break this circular wait, even-numbered coders take their left
  dongle first then their right, while odd-numbered coders take their right
  dongle first then their left. This asymmetry prevents the cycle from forming.

- **Starvation prevention.** Without arbitration, the fastest coder keeps
  re-acquiring the same dongles and starves its neighbours. Each dongle owns a
  priority queue: a coder registers a request and is only served when it reaches
  the front of the queue. In FIFO mode, requests are served in arrival order; in
  EDF mode, the coder with the earliest burnout deadline
  (`last_compile_start + time_to_burnout`) is served first.

- **Cooldown handling.** After a dongle is released it stays unavailable for
  `dongle_cooldown` milliseconds. A plain `pthread_cond_wait` only wakes on a
  signal, but the end of a cooldown emits no signal — it is simply time passing.
  Waiting coders therefore use `pthread_cond_timedwait`, waking periodically to
  re-check whether the cooldown has elapsed.

- **Precise burnout detection.** A dedicated monitor thread continuously checks
  each coder's elapsed time since its last compile start. When a coder exceeds
  `time_to_burnout`, the monitor logs the burnout and stops the simulation,
  well within the 10 ms requirement.

- **Log serialization.** All state messages are printed through a single mutex
  (`print_mutex`), so two messages can never interleave on the same line.

## Thread synchronization mechanisms

The program uses `pthread_mutex_t` and `pthread_cond_t` primitives, with no
global variables — all shared state is passed through a pointer to a central
table structure.

- **One mutex per dongle** protects each dongle's state (`taken`,
  `last_release`) and guards its condition variable and priority queue.

- **One condition variable per dongle** lets waiting coders sleep instead of
  busy-waiting. When a dongle is released, a `broadcast` wakes all waiters; each
  re-checks whether it is at the front of the queue, and only the eligible one
  proceeds while the others go back to sleep.

- **One mutex per coder** (`state_mutex`) protects `last_compile_start` and
  `compil_count`. This prevents a data race between the coder writing these
  values and the monitor reading them: every access goes through a thread-safe
  getter or setter that locks, reads or writes, then unlocks.

- **`print_mutex`** serializes all logging output.

- **`stop_mutex`** protects the global stop flag, read by every coder and the
  monitor and written by the monitor.

- **`seq_mutex`** protects the FIFO sequence counter, guaranteeing that two
  coders requesting a dongle at nearly the same time receive distinct,
  increasing sequence numbers.

- **Priority queue (binary min-heap).** Each dongle uses a custom min-heap that
  orders requests by a `key`: the sequence number in FIFO mode, the burnout
  deadline in EDF mode. The smallest key is always at the root, so the same heap
  serves both scheduling policies — only the key changes.

